{
  "case_id": "2001-01-682",
  "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Lawrence S. Robbins (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Eighth Circuit held in this case that a\nprivate plaintiff may obtain punitive damages in an action\nbrought against municipal government defendants under\nsection 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and title II\nof the Americans with Disabilities Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What are we supposed to do then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States, as amicus curiae (Gregory G. Garre)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The statutes at issue in this case,\nsection 504 of the Rehabilitation Act and title II of the\nADA, do not sanction the award of punitive damages which\nare, by their definition, damages in excess of that\nnecessary to make good any wrong done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But you -- and you rely on the heavy gun in statutes like title VI, title IX. That heavy gun is so heavy that it's never used."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0001-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "it -- it's an unusual remedy and it's a harsh remedy, and simply because it's not used in more cases doesn't mean that it doesn't have a deterrent effect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, that was pointed out in the brief in opposition."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In the Rehabilitation Act amendments and in section 502 of the ADA, what Congress said expressly is that as to remedies against States, which are one of the entities entitled to the Newport presumption, they're subject to the same remedies under the statutes as are any other private or public entities, meaning if you can get it against a private entity, you can get it against a State."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We're not talking about punitive damages here as a surrogate for some component of compensatories. We're talking about it as a remedy designed to provide a deterrent that will assist in -- in Congress's goal of eliminating discrimination against persons with disabilities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And given the distinctions between the ADA and section 504, those policies are not served here by immunizing the -- the defendants against punitive damages on -- under a Newport rationale."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Mr. Nelson, last year in -- in the Alexander case and this year in the Malesko case, we've indicated that we're taking a much more critical look, I think, at these kind of claims than we ever did in Carlson."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robbins",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The 1991 act amended title I of the ADA and also the Rehabilitation Act to provide a capped punitive damages remedy available only to -- only to nongovernmental entities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Robbins",
      "case_id": "2001-01-682",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "12cc86e3b7db2203e8d3658d86db0b845acfee191f034ff4e8a410725ff50470",
      "evidence_id": "ev-12cc86e3b7db-0002-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Although title I of the ADA has a limited punitive damages provision applicable only to employment cases and exempting the government, and although title III has a civil penalties provision applicable only to public accommodations, title II, which is silent, shall have an unlimited punitive damages provision which can be applied against governmental entities."
    },
